---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditOverlayEditor from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditOverlayEditor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="add-text-to-pdf">
  <PdfEditOverlayEditor client:load mode="text" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Typing onto a PDF that has no form fields</h2>
      <p>
        Many forms are pictures of paper: a scan, a fax, or an export from a design tool that had no
        interactive fields. There is nothing to click into, so the usual advice is to print the form,
        fill it by hand and scan it back. That takes a printer and gives a worse result.
      </p>
      <p>
        With this tool, you click where a value goes, type it, position it, and download a PDF with
        real, selectable text on the printed line.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Getting text onto the line, exactly</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Zoom to 200% with the plus button so you can see the printed rule clearly.</li>
        <li>Click just above the line, near where the first letter goes.</li>
        <li>Type into the text box in the panel below. Press Enter for a second line.</li>
        <li>
          With the object focused, tap the arrow keys. Each press moves it one point (1/72 inch), and
          Shift with an arrow moves ten. Two or three presses usually move text from "floating" to
          "written on the line".
        </li>
        <li>Match the size to the surrounding print: 10 pt for most forms, 11–12 pt for large-print forms, 8–9 pt for dense tables.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Which font to pick</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[600px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">The form looks like</th>
              <th scope="col">Choose</th>
              <th scope="col">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Arial or Helvetica</td>
              <td>Helvetica</td>
              <td>Metrically near-identical to Arial; the default for government forms</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Times New Roman or a book face</td>
              <td>Times Roman</td>
              <td>Same proportions, same look at small sizes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Typewritten or monospaced</td>
              <td>Courier</td>
              <td>Fixed pitch, lines up in boxed character grids</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Handwritten original</td>
              <td>Times Italic</td>
              <td>Softer than a sans face; or draw it with <a href="/edit-pdf">Edit PDF</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        All eight faces are among the fourteen fonts that each PDF reader must have built in. Nothing
        is embedded, the file grows only by the bytes of your text, and it renders the same on all
        devices.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The character-set limit, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>
        The built-in fonts cover WinAnsi, the Western European set: A–Z, digits, punctuation, and
        accented Latin letters such as é, ñ, ü, ç and å. It does not include Polish ł, Turkish ğ, Greek,
        Cyrillic, Hebrew, Arabic, Devanagari, Chinese, Japanese or Korean, or the curly quotes that
        some word processors insert automatically.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tool does not write a file in which those characters become blanks. It names the
        unsupported character and stops. As a workaround, render the text as a transparent PNG in an
        image editor and place it with <a href="/add-image-to-pdf">Add Image to PDF</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Filling a boxed character grid</h2>
      <p>
        Forms with one small box per character (common on visa, bank and tax forms) need Courier and
        some arithmetic. To measure the pitch of the boxes, place a character in the first and last box
        of a run and read the coordinates in the panel. Divide the distance by the number of boxes to
        get the spacing. Then place each character as its own text object at that spacing. This takes
        time, but a scanner can read the result, which is often not true of handwriting.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>If the PDF does have fields</h2>
      <p>
        Try <a href="/fill-pdf-form">Fill Out PDF Forms</a> first. It detects AcroForm fields, shows an
        input on each one, and saves an editable or a flattened copy. This is faster and more reliable
        than free text on a real form. Come back here when that tool reports no fields.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
